          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Livius</strong><br>\nLivius, also known as Titus Livius, was born in 59 BC in Patavium, now known as Padua, in northern Italy. Despite living during the turbulent times that saw the fall of the Republican government and the rise of the Imperial age in Rome, much of Livius' life is shrouded in mystery. We do not have detailed records of his early years or his education, but it is evident that he was well-educated, given his later works.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Livius' Roman Context</strong><br>\nDuring Livius' lifetime, Rome was transforming from a republic with elected leaders into an empire under the rule of one man. This period was marked by civil wars and the rise of notable figures such as Julius Caesar, Augustus, and the members of the Second Triumvirate. Livius lived through the end of the Republic and the establishment of the Empire, which deeply influenced his historical writings.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Livius as a Historian</strong><br>\nLivius began writing during the reign of Augustus, Rome's first Emperor. He is most famous for his work \"Ab Urbe Condita,\" translated as \"From the Founding of the City,\" a monumental history of Rome from its legendary beginnings until his own times. Although not all of Livius' writings survive to this day, the preserved portions provide a comprehensive view of Rome's past.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
