            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Writing Style and Purpose</strong><br>\nLivius' writing style is often characterized as both elaborate and moralistic. He wrote in a narrative form that aimed to offer moral lessons from history. His purpose was not just to record the events but to teach future generations through the examples of Rome's forebears, portraying their virtues and vices.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Ab Urbe Condita\"</strong><br>\nAb Urbe Condita originally consisted of 142 books, of which only 35 are entirely preserved. These books cover the history from the earliest days of Rome up to 9 BC. Despite its gaps, this work remains one of the most important sources for early Roman history and the Republican era.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Roman Morality</strong><br>\nLivius' work was a tool for promoting the moral revitalization of Rome. He believed that Rome's greatness was rooted in the moral strength of its ancestors. By highlighting this in his narratives, he hoped to influence the Roman citizens and leadership to return to the traditional values that he felt were responsible for Rome's success.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
